  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1100 AM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... As the remnants of Isaias track through the \nTennessee Valley and push tropical moisture northeastward, Central \nNC will experience unsettling weather with showers and storms from \nSaturday afternoon/evening through Sunday night.\n\nRain chances increase Saturday afternoon, with widespread rainfall \ndriven by strong isentropic lift peaking between Saturday evening \nand Sunday. Models show a brief lull midday Sunday before a final \nbroken band of showers and storms passes through Sunday afternoon \nand evening. Conditions will clear rapidly from west to east \novernight into Monday morning.\n\nStorm totals across Central NC will average 1 to 2 inches, though \nlocalized urban or poor-drainage flooding is possible. Cold Air \nDamming set up will keep totals lower across the NW Piedmont (~0.5 \nto 1 inch), while a secondary low development along the coast will \nresult in localized higher amounts across the eastern Piedmont and \nCoastal Plain.\n\nThe severe threat remains low overall due to limited instability in \na high-shear, Low-CAPE environment. However, a few isolated strong \nto severe storms capable of damaging wind gusts cannot be ruled out \nSunday afternoon/evening, primarily across the far eastern and \nsoutheastern areas.\n\nThe CAD wedge will keep conditions cool and damp over the weekend, \nwith highs in the mid/upper 60s NW to low 70s in the SE and breezy \neasterly flow before sunshine returns Monday afternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Unseasonably warm, with little to no chance of \nrain, next week\n\nIn the mid/upr-levels, a sub-tropical ridge will develop and \nstrengthen across the w-cntl Gulf Coast through the middle of next \nweek, then progress ewd and across and offshore the Southeast. The \nmodels suggest it will do so downstream of a complex, srn stream-\ndominated trough initially over the Intermountain West, which will \nbecome increasingly influenced by/phased with a nrn stream trough \nforecast to amplify from the Gulf of AK to the upr MS Valley/upr \nGreat Lakes through the same time. Models diverge with the amplitude \nand timing of that trough as it progresses across the ern US next \nweekend, with related decreasing forecast confidence.\n\nAt the surface, high pressure will extend across the Southeast \nthroughout the week. There have been indications in recent days' \nmodel guidance that a moisture-starved backdoor cold front will \nsettle across the Middle Atlantic during the middle of the week, \nthen stall and retreat nwd ahead of a cyclone and frontal system \napproaching with, and related to the aforementioned trough aloft. \nWhile it remains unclear to what extent that backdoor front may \nprogress into cntl NC, any associated influence and post-frontal \ncooling would be favored and mostly confined to Thu. Should that \nboundary remain to the north of cntl NC, it's possible that the \nunseasonably warm pattern that will otherwise extend across the \nSoutheast would support warmer-than-forecast and near record high \ntemperatures in cntl NC mid to late next week.",
